WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.315 --> 00:00:03.232
(swooshing)

00:00:03.232 --> 00:00:04.065
- Practice.

00:00:04.065 --> 00:00:04.898
- Practice.

00:00:04.898 --> 00:00:05.731
- Practice.

00:00:05.731 --> 00:00:06.564
- Practice.

00:00:06.564 --> 00:00:07.397
- Practice.

00:00:07.397 --> 00:00:08.574
- What's up Believe Nation.

00:00:08.574 --> 00:00:10.360
Today we're going to
give you some motivation

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.594
so you can practice, practice, practice.

00:00:13.594 --> 00:00:15.339
And as always, you know the drill,

00:00:15.339 --> 00:00:17.514
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:17.514 --> 00:00:19.793
leave it down in the
comments, put quotes around it

00:00:19.793 --> 00:00:21.899
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:22.732
Enjoy.

00:00:23.704 --> 00:00:27.037
(dramatic string music)

00:00:34.593 --> 00:00:35.760
- You look for

00:00:37.565 --> 00:00:39.934
that thing that moves you.

00:00:39.934 --> 00:00:41.610
I don't know what it is.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:43.582
I like repetition.

00:00:43.582 --> 00:00:44.811
There's a saying goes,

00:00:44.811 --> 00:00:47.144
"Repetition keeps me green."

00:00:48.387 --> 00:00:49.220
- [Charlie] Yeah.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:50.395
- I love that saying, because--

00:00:50.395 --> 00:00:51.329
- [Charlie] Repetition keeps me green.

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:53.463
- Keeps me green, fresh.

00:00:53.463 --> 00:00:55.549
- Because the idea

00:00:55.549 --> 00:00:58.748
that we do performances
over and over again,

00:00:58.748 --> 00:01:00.974
you say, well doesn't
that get boring or stale,

00:01:00.974 --> 00:01:02.035
but no!

00:01:02.035 --> 00:01:05.217
It's in the repetition
that the creation comes,

00:01:05.217 --> 00:01:06.849
that the expression comes.

00:01:06.849 --> 00:01:08.179
Because, I'll give you an example.

00:01:08.179 --> 00:01:09.752
I was doing Richard, once,

00:01:09.752 --> 00:01:11.698
not particularly well,

00:01:11.698 --> 00:01:13.347
and the show is going on,

00:01:13.347 --> 00:01:16.595
and we couldn't get this court scene

00:01:16.595 --> 00:01:17.694
with a lot of people in it.

00:01:17.694 --> 00:01:19.803
I didn't understand what
I was doing in the scene.

00:01:19.803 --> 00:01:21.716
So we would call rehearsals,

00:01:21.716 --> 00:01:23.857
we'd rehearse it and stuff,

00:01:23.857 --> 00:01:26.108
and we would talk about it,
think about it, you know,

00:01:26.108 --> 00:01:29.853
like all this kind of
work were people together

00:01:29.853 --> 00:01:32.245
are trying to figure out something.

00:01:32.245 --> 00:01:34.912
The 85th performance of Richard,

00:01:36.526 --> 00:01:38.605
my 85th entrance,

00:01:38.605 --> 00:01:41.023
I knew the court scene.

00:01:41.023 --> 00:01:42.494
I understood it.

00:01:42.494 --> 00:01:44.229
I was there. I could play it.

00:01:44.229 --> 00:01:45.422
- [Charlie] But not in 84.

00:01:45.422 --> 00:01:46.897
- Not in 84.

00:01:46.897 --> 00:01:49.369
I might have been getting
close and didn't realize it.

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:50.202
I gave up the ghost, I guess.

00:01:50.202 --> 00:01:51.035
I just gave it up and said, "Oh, screw it.

00:01:51.035 --> 00:01:54.943
"It's not going to happen.
I'll just keep going on."

00:01:54.943 --> 00:01:56.072
And I found it.

00:01:56.072 --> 00:01:59.239
- I remain uninterested in that topic.

00:02:00.701 --> 00:02:01.880
- [Charlie] Which one?

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.856
The relation between gift and practice?

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:06.757
- No, I'm uninterested in natural gifts.

00:02:06.757 --> 00:02:10.489
I know they exist and I know
there's such a thing as talent,

00:02:10.489 --> 00:02:12.513
I just feel like...

00:02:12.513 --> 00:02:13.888
And so what, right?

00:02:13.888 --> 00:02:15.509
Because there are people,

00:02:15.509 --> 00:02:16.993
and I always think of,
you're a basketball fan,

00:02:16.993 --> 00:02:18.340
I always think of Derrick Coleman.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:19.456
- Yes.

00:02:19.456 --> 00:02:21.893
- I remember chatting with a
guy who worked for the Sixers,

00:02:21.893 --> 00:02:23.636
and he said, "You have to understand

00:02:23.636 --> 00:02:26.837
"that Derrick Coleman was
the most gifted man ever

00:02:26.837 --> 00:02:29.033
"to set foot on a basketball court."

00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:32.174
He said, "You've heard
a million other names,

00:02:32.174 --> 00:02:34.655
"nobody was as good as Derrick."

00:02:34.655 --> 00:02:36.524
And like, who has heard today, who thinks,

00:02:36.524 --> 00:02:38.717
who puts Derrick Coleman
up there in the Pantheon?

00:02:38.717 --> 00:02:39.550
No one.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:40.729
He didn't want to work.

00:02:40.729 --> 00:02:41.968
He had bad habits.

00:02:41.968 --> 00:02:43.155
He had a bad attitude.

00:02:43.155 --> 00:02:44.591
I mean, so what?

00:02:44.591 --> 00:02:47.728
Give me somebody who wants to
go to practice in the morning

00:02:47.728 --> 00:02:48.904
and who wants to try in games,

00:02:48.904 --> 00:02:50.432
and I'll want to celebrate him.

00:02:50.432 --> 00:02:52.805
- It wasn't until I started my own team

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.787
when I realized that there are people

00:02:55.787 --> 00:02:59.562
with incredible talent and no motivation.

00:02:59.562 --> 00:03:00.705
And then there are other people

00:03:00.705 --> 00:03:02.855
that are willing to work their ass off

00:03:02.855 --> 00:03:05.298
who don't have a natural ability,

00:03:05.298 --> 00:03:09.333
but will become successful
because they're so determined.

00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:13.739
And I saw it especially
in a couple of guys

00:03:13.739 --> 00:03:16.257
that I first put on the Birdhouse team.

00:03:16.257 --> 00:03:19.128
Namely, Andrew Reynolds, Matt Beach.

00:03:19.128 --> 00:03:21.378
Matt Beach was, in my eyes,

00:03:22.312 --> 00:03:24.056
the most naturally talented skateboarder

00:03:24.056 --> 00:03:25.311
that I've ever seen.

00:03:25.311 --> 00:03:26.995
Everything came easy to him,

00:03:26.995 --> 00:03:30.174
and when I would request
these guys to go on tour,

00:03:30.174 --> 00:03:32.454
all I ask of them is that they skate

00:03:32.454 --> 00:03:33.829
for exhibitions and whatnot.

00:03:33.829 --> 00:03:35.357
Matt wouldn't skate half the time.

00:03:35.357 --> 00:03:36.537
I flew him to Europe,

00:03:36.537 --> 00:03:38.171
I didn't have any money then

00:03:38.171 --> 00:03:39.222
in our company,

00:03:39.222 --> 00:03:41.405
but I was flying these guys
to Europe for competitions.

00:03:41.405 --> 00:03:43.710
He would go to the contest
and not feel like skating.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.079
And I was like, how can you do that?

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:48.503
Not just to me, but to yourself.

00:03:48.503 --> 00:03:52.526
You have this incredible
talent and you just don't care.

00:03:52.526 --> 00:03:53.983
And then I saw Andrew Reynolds,

00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:56.372
who was this scrappy little guy,

00:03:56.372 --> 00:03:59.568
not very clean in terms of his style,

00:03:59.568 --> 00:04:01.065
but trying some of the hardest stuff,

00:04:01.065 --> 00:04:02.509
taking the heaviest hits,

00:04:02.509 --> 00:04:05.523
but always skating his
ass off and always trying,

00:04:05.523 --> 00:04:06.975
and he has become probably

00:04:06.975 --> 00:04:09.262
the most well-respected
street skater today.

00:04:09.262 --> 00:04:10.095
Andrew Reynolds.

00:04:10.095 --> 00:04:13.258
I mean, he formed Baker
Brand, Deathwish Skateboards,

00:04:13.258 --> 00:04:17.672
the community of the most
hardcore skaters these days

00:04:17.672 --> 00:04:19.784
call him the boss.

00:04:19.784 --> 00:04:22.165
Because he developed his style.

00:04:22.165 --> 00:04:23.954
Because he worked his ass off.

00:04:23.954 --> 00:04:27.025
And he's the shining example of hard work.

00:04:27.025 --> 00:04:28.678
- People have asked me
that over the years,

00:04:28.678 --> 00:04:31.554
you know, how do I curl the
ball, or how do I bend the ball,

00:04:31.554 --> 00:04:33.150
and it's something that just happened.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.308
It's not something that

00:04:35.308 --> 00:04:37.405
I thought, OK, I want to bend the ball

00:04:37.405 --> 00:04:38.918
better than anyone else,

00:04:38.918 --> 00:04:41.448
so I'm going to kick the
ball in this certain way.

00:04:41.448 --> 00:04:44.065
I just worked on my striking of the ball.

00:04:44.065 --> 00:04:45.335
I just practiced every day.

00:04:45.335 --> 00:04:49.367
It's something that I've
done from a very young age.

00:04:49.367 --> 00:04:51.868
Even after every training session

00:04:51.868 --> 00:04:56.134
I take balls on my own
and just curl and kick

00:04:56.134 --> 00:05:00.421
and try and find new ways of
getting the ball in the net

00:05:00.421 --> 00:05:01.729
without anyone touching it,

00:05:01.729 --> 00:05:05.454
and I've been lucky enough to
have done that over the years

00:05:05.454 --> 00:05:07.295
with the way I kick the ball,

00:05:07.295 --> 00:05:10.003
and I know that it's a unique style,

00:05:10.003 --> 00:05:14.047
I know it's a unique style
just because my back hurts

00:05:14.047 --> 00:05:17.672
a little bit more than it
did when I was 15 years old,

00:05:17.672 --> 00:05:21.873
but no, it's something
I practice, of course,

00:05:21.873 --> 00:05:24.023
that's one thing I tell kids.

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:25.360
Unless you practice,

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.958
you're never going to
get better at something.

00:05:28.958 --> 00:05:30.557
- You have to practice over the years.

00:05:30.557 --> 00:05:31.913
I wasn't great in the beginning.

00:05:31.913 --> 00:05:33.263
I was aiight.

00:05:33.263 --> 00:05:34.374
And then I got cool.

00:05:34.374 --> 00:05:35.640
And then I got good.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.183
Then I got better.

00:05:37.183 --> 00:05:39.202
And then I got great.

00:05:39.202 --> 00:05:41.793
Those are steps to becoming great

00:05:41.793 --> 00:05:42.922
that a lot of people ain't willin

00:05:42.922 --> 00:05:45.095
to take those steps.

00:05:45.095 --> 00:05:48.550
It takes ten years at
something, continuously,

00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:49.973
to become great at it.

00:05:49.973 --> 00:05:52.129
And I stuck with it for ten years straight

00:05:52.129 --> 00:05:53.328
before I had a record deal,

00:05:53.328 --> 00:05:54.340
before I had money,

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.296
before I had lights and camera,

00:05:56.296 --> 00:05:58.256
before I had, you know,

00:05:58.256 --> 00:06:00.162
people wanting to sit
down and interview me.

00:06:00.162 --> 00:06:01.662
- I need to practice.

00:06:01.662 --> 00:06:03.829
Member before the day
comes, you got to practice.

00:06:03.829 --> 00:06:06.842
You cannot be standing
at the free throw line

00:06:06.842 --> 00:06:08.709
playing professional basketball

00:06:08.709 --> 00:06:11.325
in the seventh game of
the NBA Championship

00:06:11.325 --> 00:06:14.104
and hope right then you're
going to be practiced.

00:06:14.104 --> 00:06:16.422
Practice is too late.

00:06:16.422 --> 00:06:19.012
Larry Bird, the great basketball player,

00:06:19.012 --> 00:06:22.630
he used to shoot, whatever,
a thousand free throws,

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:23.902
eight in the morning.

00:06:23.902 --> 00:06:25.346
My friend Don Ravine

00:06:25.346 --> 00:06:27.304
trains a lot of other
pro basketball players.

00:06:27.304 --> 00:06:29.444
LeBron James, he trains.

00:06:29.444 --> 00:06:30.277
He trains

00:06:32.498 --> 00:06:34.003
Durant.

00:06:34.003 --> 00:06:35.487
Kobe.

00:06:35.487 --> 00:06:36.743
Chris Paul.

00:06:36.743 --> 00:06:38.084
And he says, these guys are up

00:06:38.084 --> 00:06:39.504
when everybody else is sleeping.

00:06:39.504 --> 00:06:41.061
They're practicing.

00:06:41.061 --> 00:06:43.953
So, everything you want is going to entail

00:06:43.953 --> 00:06:45.529
a good bit of practice.

00:06:45.529 --> 00:06:47.078
- I always believe that natural talent

00:06:47.078 --> 00:06:48.818
can take you so far,

00:06:48.818 --> 00:06:51.824
but I think you need
to have the work ethic

00:06:51.824 --> 00:06:55.157
and the drive to achieve your potential.

00:06:56.073 --> 00:06:58.735
I'd say my natural talent

00:06:58.735 --> 00:07:02.068
probably brought me 50% of the way,

00:07:03.734 --> 00:07:06.351
but then after that it was all down

00:07:06.351 --> 00:07:07.415
to the work that I've done

00:07:07.415 --> 00:07:08.780
and the volume of work that I've done

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.378
over the last 15 years

00:07:11.378 --> 00:07:13.904
when I really started
to take golf seriously

00:07:13.904 --> 00:07:16.651
and thought about making this a career.

00:07:16.651 --> 00:07:19.063
- Whenever you talk about
ethics and integrity

00:07:19.063 --> 00:07:20.304
in the Indian context,

00:07:20.304 --> 00:07:22.130
the two companies that come to mind

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:23.926
are, one is Akartasans,

00:07:23.926 --> 00:07:25.350
and the other is Wipro.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:27.588
What is so special about
these two companies

00:07:27.588 --> 00:07:30.040
that has been imbibed
in these organizations?

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:31.290
How is it done?

00:07:32.887 --> 00:07:36.127
- Very simple, it's done by practice.

00:07:36.127 --> 00:07:38.682
There's no other way you
imbibe things in an organize...

00:07:38.682 --> 00:07:39.699
It is practice.

00:07:39.699 --> 00:07:40.732
Integrity is practice,

00:07:40.732 --> 00:07:43.235
leadership is practice,

00:07:43.235 --> 00:07:44.556
innovation is practice,

00:07:44.556 --> 00:07:46.133
it's all practice.

00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:48.267
You've got to figure out what
you want your company to be

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:50.237
and what you consider
as non-compromisable,

00:07:50.237 --> 00:07:52.053
not negotiable,

00:07:52.053 --> 00:07:55.002
and then set those out and
keep them as simple as possible

00:07:55.002 --> 00:07:56.970
and keep them as few as possible,

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:58.063
and practice them.

00:07:58.063 --> 00:08:01.468
And practice them every day,
every hour, every minute

00:08:01.468 --> 00:08:03.831
and have zero exception to them.

00:08:03.831 --> 00:08:05.835
- The advice I give to any young kid

00:08:05.835 --> 00:08:08.671
is, anyone can play a game of football.

00:08:08.671 --> 00:08:09.951
Anyone.

00:08:09.951 --> 00:08:12.508
But practice makes you a real footballer.

00:08:12.508 --> 00:08:14.561
You see games in a Sunday,

00:08:14.561 --> 00:08:17.779
pub games, oh, love a game of football.

00:08:17.779 --> 00:08:21.950
The real players practice
after they go back home.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:25.755
I remember being once in
Glasglow many, many years ago

00:08:25.755 --> 00:08:28.265
and Gary Player was the guest--

00:08:28.265 --> 00:08:30.132
- [Male Voice] The golfer?
- Yeah, the golfer.

00:08:30.132 --> 00:08:32.950
And they asked him the question,

00:08:32.950 --> 00:08:36.085
"Why do you keep practicin'
from the bunker?"

00:08:36.085 --> 00:08:37.778
Because he was famous for the bunker.

00:08:37.778 --> 00:08:41.164
He says, "Well, I know I'm
going to be in the bunker

00:08:41.164 --> 00:08:43.478
"at one time in a game,
maybe twice in a game,

00:08:43.478 --> 00:08:45.845
"and I've got to put in a hole.

00:08:45.845 --> 00:08:48.376
"That's why I practice and
practice and practice."

00:08:48.376 --> 00:08:51.305
That's a great example
of what you have to do

00:08:51.305 --> 00:08:52.667
to be a top player.

00:08:52.667 --> 00:08:54.486
- [Announcer] The Bulls
can win it right here.

00:08:54.486 --> 00:08:55.697
The Bulls can win it.

00:08:55.697 --> 00:08:58.695
Unbelievable! 13 seconds left.

00:08:58.695 --> 00:09:00.346
Jordan, left side.

00:09:00.346 --> 00:09:02.096
- I practice as if I'm playing in a game

00:09:02.096 --> 00:09:03.806
so when the moment comes in the game,

00:09:03.806 --> 00:09:05.253
it's not new to me.

00:09:05.253 --> 00:09:07.276
That's the beauty of
the game of basketball.

00:09:07.276 --> 00:09:08.729
That's the reason why you practice.

00:09:08.729 --> 00:09:09.682
That's the effort.

00:09:09.682 --> 00:09:12.511
So when you get to that moment,
you don't have to think.

00:09:12.511 --> 00:09:14.298
Instinctively things happen.

00:09:14.298 --> 00:09:16.548
- [Announcer] Jordan a try.

00:09:19.938 --> 00:09:21.605
Aims. Fires. Scores!

00:09:22.793 --> 00:09:24.543
The Bulls lead 87-86!

00:09:26.297 --> 00:09:28.797
Michael Jordan with 45 points.

00:09:30.057 --> 00:09:30.890
(cheering)

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:33.823
(dramatic piano music)

00:09:33.823 --> 00:09:36.666
- I think it was a very defining moment

00:09:36.666 --> 00:09:39.078
of what my career was in Chicago.

00:09:39.078 --> 00:09:41.026
- [Announcer] The Chicago Bulls

00:09:41.026 --> 00:09:44.193
have won their sixth NBA championship!

00:09:49.077 --> 00:09:50.880
- [Announcer] And that's the last image

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:52.150
of Michael Jordan.

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:53.983
How magnificent is it.

00:09:56.671 --> 00:09:57.915
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:09:57.915 --> 00:09:58.929
I hope you enjoyed.

00:09:58.929 --> 00:10:00.763
I love to know, what did you think?

00:10:00.763 --> 00:10:01.596
What did you take from this?

00:10:01.596 --> 00:10:02.429
Are you going to work harder?

00:10:02.429 --> 00:10:03.412
Are you going to practice more?

00:10:03.412 --> 00:10:04.942
Are you going to hustle more?

00:10:04.942 --> 00:10:06.099
Leave in the comments below,

00:10:06.099 --> 00:10:07.408
what was your favorite clip,

00:10:07.408 --> 00:10:08.485
and what did you learn from it

00:10:08.485 --> 00:10:11.547
that you're going to immediately
apply to your business

00:10:11.547 --> 00:10:12.488
or life?

00:10:12.488 --> 00:10:15.544
Leave it in the comments, I'm
going to join in the discussion.

00:10:15.544 --> 00:10:17.906
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Himanshu Premi,

00:10:17.906 --> 00:10:20.131
thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book

00:10:20.131 --> 00:10:22.723
Your One Word and making
that awesome YouTube video

00:10:22.723 --> 00:10:23.690
around it as well.

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:26.058
I really really really
really appreciate it.

00:10:26.058 --> 00:10:28.788
- I encourage everyone to read this book,

00:10:28.788 --> 00:10:30.818
because this book has actually helped me

00:10:30.818 --> 00:10:32.713
to find my one word,

00:10:32.713 --> 00:10:35.554
and I'm 100% sure that it'll definitely

00:10:35.554 --> 00:10:37.887
help you to find your one word.

00:10:37.887 --> 00:10:39.569
So thank you guys again for watching,

00:10:39.569 --> 00:10:40.741
I believe in you.

00:10:40.741 --> 00:10:43.189
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:10:43.189 --> 00:10:45.584
and whatever your one word is.

00:10:45.584 --> 00:10:48.367
Much love, I'll see you soon.

